---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Treadmill Workout Plan (2026): A 12-Week Progressive Program";
const description =
  "A 12-week treadmill workout plan with walker, beginner-runner and runner tracks: week-by-week speed, incline, duration and RPE, plus deloads and strength days.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Workouts & Training", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/training/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Workout Plan", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-workout-plan/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a good treadmill workout plan for beginners?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Three sessions a week for the first four weeks, mostly easy walking at a conversational effort, adding about five minutes per session every week or two and nothing else. From week five add a fourth session, then alternate small increases in duration with small increases in incline, and make every fourth week lighter. Beginners who want to run should use walk-run intervals at 4.5 to 5.5 mph inside a fixed 30-minute session.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many days a week should I use a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Three days is a sound start and four is a good target for most people. The US guidelines recommend 150 to 300 minutes of moderate activity or 75 to 150 minutes of vigorous activity a week, spread across the week, plus muscle-strengthening work on at least two days. Four sessions of 35 to 45 minutes reaches the lower end comfortably.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best treadmill routine for weight loss?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Mostly incline walking, for more minutes than you think, sustained for months. The ACSM's 2009 position stand associated fewer than 150 minutes a week with minimal weight change, 150 to 250 minutes with modest loss, and more than about 250 minutes with clinically significant loss, usually alongside diet changes. Build toward that gradually, keep hard sessions to one a week, keep two strength days, and do not eat back the console's calorie estimate.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the 10% rule for increasing training real?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is a reasonable ceiling but a weak law. A randomised trial of novice runners found a programme built on the 10% rule produced almost the same injury rate as a faster standard programme, about 20% in both groups. A later cohort study suggested that jumps of more than 30% over two weeks may raise the risk of some overuse injuries. Avoid big jumps, and at very low volumes add a few minutes per session rather than calculating percentages.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a deload week and do I need one?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A deload week is a planned lighter week, usually every third or fourth week, with the same number of sessions at roughly two-thirds to three-quarters of the previous week's minutes. It lets accumulated fatigue clear while fitness remains, and most people feel better the week after. Keep the sessions and a little of the intensity; just make everything shorter.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I do strength training on the same day as a treadmill workout?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Pair strength work with easy treadmill days, either after the session or on its own day. Avoid hard intervals or hills immediately before heavy lower-body lifting, and avoid heavy leg work the day before a hard treadmill session. Two or three short whole-body sessions a week meets the guidelines.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What should I do if a week of the plan feels too hard?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Repeat it. Repeat a week if you could not finish its sessions as written, if the effort ran one or two points higher than the target, if soreness lasted into the next session, or if you missed more than one session. Go back two weeks after an illness or a two-week break. Pain that is sharp, in a joint, or that changes how you move is a reason to see a clinician rather than to repeat.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-20",
      dateModified: "2026-10-20",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-20",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-20",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill workout plan",
      "treadmill routine for weight loss",
      "treadmill cardio workout",
      "treadmill training plan",
      "12-week treadmill plan",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See the Week-by-Week Plan", link: "#foundation" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-treadmillworkoutplan" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-treadmillworkoutplan)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Training Plan
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          A 12-Week <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Treadmill Workout Plan</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          A single good session is easy to find. What most people lack is the sequence: what to do in week one, what changes in week five, and when to back off. This plan gives you twelve weeks of it, in three tracks, with every speed, grade and duration written down.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#foundation" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Weeks 1–4
          </a>
          <a href="#progression" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Weeks 5–12
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 20, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What this plan is, and is not.</strong> We review
          treadmills. This is a general training structure for healthy adults, built on the US
          physical activity guidelines; it is not medical advice, physiotherapy, coaching or
          nutrition advice. If you have a heart, lung, metabolic or joint condition, take
          medication that affects heart rate, are pregnant, or have been inactive for a long time,
          talk to your doctor before starting. Stop and seek advice for chest pain, unusual
          breathlessness, dizziness, an irregular heartbeat or pain that changes how you walk.
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the numbers:</strong> speeds and grades are typical
          starting points for illustration. The effort column (RPE) is the instruction; the speed
          column is a guess at where that effort usually sits.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. The Short Answer: The Plan at a Glance</a>
          <a href="#guidelines" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. What the Guidelines Ask For, and Where This Plan Lands</a>
          <a href="#choose-track" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Choose Your Track (and How to Read the Tables)</a>
          <a href="#foundation" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Weeks 1–4: The Foundation Block</a>
          <a href="#progression" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Weeks 5–12: The Eight-Week Progression</a>
          <a href="#weekly-layout" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Laying Out the Week, Strength Days Included</a>
          <a href="#progression-rules" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. The Progression Rules, and the Limits of the 10% Rule</a>
          <a href="#deloads" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Deload Weeks: Why Weeks 4, 8 and 12 Are Lighter</a>
          <a href="#repeat-a-week" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. When to Repeat a Week (or Go Back Two)</a>
          <a href="#weight-loss" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Adapting the Plan for Weight Loss</a>
          <a href="#sessions" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. The Sessions in the Plan, and Where Each Is Explained</a>
          <a href="#after-week-12" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. After Week 12</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">13. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A treadmill workout plan is not a list of good sessions. It is a decision about
          order — which session comes first, what changes each week, how much, and when you
          deliberately do less. Get the order right and ordinary sessions add up to real fitness;
          get it wrong and excellent sessions end in sore shins by week three.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Short Answer: The Plan at a Glance</h2>
<p>
            This <strong>treadmill workout plan</strong> runs for twelve weeks: a
            <strong>four-week foundation block</strong> that builds the habit and the tissues, then
            an <strong>eight-week progression</strong> that takes you to, or past, the 150 weekly
            minutes of moderate activity the US guidelines recommend. There are three tracks, and
            you follow one:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8">
<table class="min-w-[620px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
<thead><tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white text-left"><th class="px-3 py-3 font-black">Track</th><th class="px-3 py-3 font-black">Who it is for</th><th class="px-3 py-3 font-black">Weeks 1–4</th><th class="px-3 py-3 font-black">Where week 11 lands</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class=""><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top font-bold text-gray-900">Walker</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">You can walk 20 minutes comfortably and have no running goal</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">3 walks a week, 75 → 100 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">4 walks, 160 min, including 25 min of incline work at 6%</td></tr>
<tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top font-bold text-gray-900">Beginner runner</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">You can walk 30 minutes briskly but cannot jog more than a few minutes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">3 run-walks, about 90 min, plus an optional incline walk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">20 minutes of continuous jogging, three times a week, plus an incline walk</td></tr>
<tr class=""><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top font-bold text-gray-900">Runner</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">You already run 20 to 30 minutes continuously, two or three times a week</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">3 easy runs, 100 → 105 min, one becoming a hill session</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">4 sessions, 160 min: easy, quality, incline walk, long run</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
          <p>
            Every track follows the same rules. <strong>Change one thing per week</strong> —
            frequency, duration or intensity, in roughly that order. <strong>Keep most of the week
            easy</strong>: about four-fifths of the minutes are conversational. <strong>Weeks 4, 8
            and 12 are lighter</strong> on purpose. And <strong>repeat a week</strong> whenever the
            one you just finished did not feel finished. The tables are below; the reasoning
            behind each rule follows them.
          </p>
          <p>
            This page is the plan. The individual sessions it calls for — easy walks, incline
            walks, intervals, hills, long runs — each have their own page on this site, linked in
            the session section, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workouts</a>
            pillar explains the four session types the plan is built from.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="guidelines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What the Guidelines Ask For, and Where This Plan Lands</h2>
<p>
            The target the plan builds toward is not ours. The <strong>Physical Activity Guidelines
            for Americans</strong> (US Department of Health and Human Services, second edition,
            2018), which the CDC summarises for the public, recommend that adults get
            <strong>150 to 300 minutes a week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity, or 75 to 150
            minutes of vigorous activity</strong>, or an equivalent mix, preferably spread across
            the week. They note additional benefits beyond 300 minutes, and add
            <strong>muscle-strengthening activity on at least two days a week</strong> covering
            the major muscle groups. The American College of Sports Medicine's 2011 position stand
            (Garber and colleagues, <em>Medicine &amp; Science in Sports &amp; Exercise</em>)
            gives the same 150-minute floor — framed as 30 minutes on five days — and recommends
            training each major muscle group two or three days a week.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two features of those guidelines shape the plan directly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vigorous minutes count double.</strong> In the guidelines' arithmetic, one
            minute of vigorous activity is worth about two of moderate. That is why the runner
            track reaches the target with fewer sessions than the walker track, and why the
            walker track's steepest incline blocks matter more than their minutes suggest.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Intensity has a definition.</strong> The guidelines classify activity of 3.0
            to 5.9 <a href="/what-are-mets-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>METs</a> (multiples of resting energy use) as moderate and 6 or more as vigorous.
            Walking at 3 mph on the flat is a little over 3 METs by the ACSM walking equation, so
            it counts; a slow stroll does not. For a felt measure, the CDC offers two: on a 0 to
            10 effort scale, moderate is a 5 or 6 and vigorous begins at 7 or 8, and in the talk
            test, moderate means you can talk but not sing, while vigorous means you cannot say
            more than a few words without pausing for breath.
          </p>
          <p>
            So where does the plan land? The walker track starts at 75 minutes a week — half the
            target, deliberately — reaches 145 minutes by week 7 and 160 by week 10. The beginner
            runner track sits at 120 minutes of activity, of which up to an hour is jogging; if
            your jogging feels vigorous (RPE 7 or above) that clears the target easily, and if it
            feels moderate, one optional 30-minute walk takes you past 150 by any count. The
            runner track starts above the target and stays there. None of the tracks goes near
            300 minutes, because a first twelve weeks is not the place to chase the upper end.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
            References: US Department of Health and Human Services, <em>Physical Activity
            Guidelines for Americans</em>, 2nd ed., 2018; CDC, "Adult Activity: An Overview" and
            "Measuring Physical Activity Intensity" (cdc.gov, checked October 20, 2026); Garber CE
            et al., ACSM position stand, <em>Med Sci Sports Exerc</em> 2011;43(7):1334–1359;
            Donnelly JE et al., ACSM position stand on weight loss, <em>Med Sci Sports Exerc</em>
            2009;41(2):459–471; Buist I et al., <em>Am J Sports Med</em> 2008;36(1):33–39;
            Nielsen RO et al., <em>J Orthop Sports Phys Ther</em> 2014;44(10):739–747.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choose-track" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Choose Your Track (and How to Read the Tables)</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Walker</strong> if you can walk 20 minutes without discomfort and running is
            not a goal, or if you are returning after a long break, carrying significant extra
            weight, or protecting a knee, hip or back. Walking with incline can take you all the
            way to the guideline target, and plenty of people never need anything else.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Beginner runner</strong> if you can walk briskly for 30 minutes and want to
            run, but cannot yet jog for more than a few minutes. If you cannot comfortably walk
            for 30 minutes yet, do the walker foundation block first and start this track
            afterwards. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-beginners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">beginner treadmill workout</a>
            page covers the very first sessions on a machine in more detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Runner</strong> if you already run 20 to 30 minutes continuously two or three
            times a week without pain. If you run more than about two and a half hours a week
            already, this plan is below your level; use its rules rather than its numbers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are between two tracks, take the lower one.</strong> Being slightly
            under-challenged for four weeks costs nothing; being over-challenged in week two is
            how plans end.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to read the tables</h3>
          <p>
            Each row is one session. The <strong>speed</strong> column is in mph and is a typical
            range, not an instruction — choose the speed inside it, or outside it, that produces
            the effort in the last column. The <strong>incline</strong> column is the treadmill's
            grade in per cent. <strong>Minutes</strong> include a five-minute easy warm-up and
            cool-down. The effort column uses a 0 to 10 rating of perceived exertion (RPE) and
            the five-zone heart-rate model from our
            <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">heart rate zones guide</a>:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8">
<table class="min-w-[600px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
<thead><tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white text-left"><th class="px-3 py-3 font-black">RPE (0–10)</th><th class="px-3 py-3 font-black">Talk test</th><th class="px-3 py-3 font-black">Zone (% of max heart rate)</th><th class="px-3 py-3 font-black">Guideline category</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class=""><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top font-bold text-gray-900">2–3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">You could sing</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Zone 1, 50–60%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Light: warm-ups, cool-downs, recovery</td></tr>
<tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top font-bold text-gray-900">4–5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Full sentences, easily</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Zone 2, 60–70%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Lower edge of moderate — keep it brisk</td></tr>
<tr class=""><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top font-bold text-gray-900">5–6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Sentences, but you could not sing</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Upper zone 2 to zone 3, 65–80%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Moderate (CDC: 5–6)</td></tr>
<tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top font-bold text-gray-900">7–8</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">A few words, then a breath</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Zone 4, 80–90%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Vigorous (CDC: from 7–8)</td></tr>
<tr class=""><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top font-bold text-gray-900">9–10</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Single words or none</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Zone 5, 90–100%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-gray-700 align-top">Not used anywhere in this plan</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
          <p>
            One honest wrinkle. The CDC's own heart-rate guidance — which, as of October 20,
            2026, no longer appears at its former address on cdc.gov — put moderate at roughly
            64 to 76% of estimated maximum and vigorous at 77 to 93%. That does not line up neatly
            with the five-zone model: an easy zone 2 session sits at the bottom edge of
            "moderate". In practice that means the easy sessions here should be brisk —
            conversational, but too much effort to sing — rather than an amble. And because every
            zone is calculated from a maximum heart rate that the 220-minus-age formula misses by
            ten beats or more for many individuals, the talk test outranks the number whenever
            they disagree. If you take a beta blocker, ignore the zone column entirely and use
            RPE.
          </p>
          <p>
            For converting the speeds to pace per mile or km/h, use our
            <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill pace and speed chart</a>.
            The ranges in this plan are taken from it: 2.8 to 3.5 mph is a comfortable-to-brisk
            walk, 4.5 to 5.5 mph the walk-jog transition and easy jog, 5 to 6 mph easy running,
            and 7 to 8 mph the interval range it gives for someone training toward a 5K.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="foundation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Weeks 1–4: The Foundation Block</h2>
<p>
            The foundation block has two jobs, and neither is fitness. The first is the habit:
            twelve sessions completed, on the days you planned, at an effort that leaves you
            wanting to come back. The second is tissue: heart and lungs adapt within two or three
            weeks, but tendons, bones and connective tissue take months, and the foundation is
            where they start to catch up. That is why the block is easier than you will want it
            to be, and why week 4 adds nothing at all.
          </p>
